17592186106000 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106638 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106000 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106000 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106000 #edb/ref 1045 "Solving 2x2 Singular Systems of Equations Using Gaussian Elimination" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106000 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186106635 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106000 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048853 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106000 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106000 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "0211c399-b12f-5dd5-8ac4-97409f9a60bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106002 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106673 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106002 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106674 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106002 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106002 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106002 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106711 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106002 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106002 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106002 #edb/ref 1045 "The Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106002 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186106673 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106002 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106002 #edb/ref 1050 840.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106002 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "e8bbfc2d-b5f4-57c0-9027-1b66b844109b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106635 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106636 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106635 #edb/ref 1061 17192 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106635 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2b66da6f-e7b3-547c-b47a-3bfe17bbc205" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106635 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107080 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106636 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106636 #edb/ref 1061 17193 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106636 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "414f018d-78f3-5c78-86b8-6363d86d3a9e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106636 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105745 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106637 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106638 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106637 #edb/ref 1061 17194 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106637 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "00d5123d-64a7-5878-9b67-ac7b7a2bf0a0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106637 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106638 #edb/ref 1061 17195 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106638 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "dc00947a-2ca5-59a7-a7a8-349b27d59bf2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106638 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107081 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106673 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106674 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106673 #edb/ref 1061 20075 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106673 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9b7139a7-3f19-5d72-857b-7a01a291e46b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106673 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106674 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106674 #edb/ref 1061 20077 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106674 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4921a5cc-f26d-5b7a-9865-e447eec1d53e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106674 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105768 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106675 #edb/ref 1061 20078 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106675 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "64cdc019-4015-5867-b014-691bcc52330a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106675 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105769 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106676 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106676 #edb/ref 1061 20081 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106676 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6e9a9912-8def-5a76-892f-0608e119d89c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106676 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105770 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106711 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106711 #edb/ref 1061 21410 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106711 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5b97f2fc-3025-5372-9dc4-19c05d084b76" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106711 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105787 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106744 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106637 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106744 #edb/ref 1061 23052 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106744 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e6eff47b-880f-55d6-94ac-af8b9767c4fd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106744 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107097 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106746 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106711 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106746 #edb/ref 1061 23514 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106746 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ab53090e-96b8-5dcc-b77f-6ff038689cac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106746 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107098 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107080 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's consider a system of linear equations where the corresponding coefficient matrix is singular. For example, consider the following system and its associated augmented matrix:\n![](images/6a/6a69daf7712312876b0ec97c540bbc472e46a7938beb8a59e694a04ad0b1d8c6.svg)\n\nTo solve the system, we can reduce $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as usual. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 6 & 15\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n![](images/a8/a8bfe3f6b27247ee9b27db8346fd94fb9068c85dec6f4a71517a821dee48dbf3.svg)\n\nSince any pair of numbers $x,y$ is a solution of the final equation $0x+0y = 0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of a single equation\n\n$$\nx - 2y = -5.\n$$\n\nFinally, we describe the **general solution** of our system.\n\n- The first variable in our equation, $x,$ is called a **basic variable**.\n\n- The second variable in our equation, $y,$ is called a **free variable**.\n\nSolving for the basic variable $(x)$ in terms of the free variable $(y),$ we get $x = -5+2y.$ So the general solution of the system is\n![](images/0b/0ba4a232f29dd6fce2a8f6d7cda39b4f5b192c20fc91fe4403dfe648231382c5.svg)\nwhere the free variable $y$ is any number.\n\nIn vector form, this solution is written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-5+2y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+y[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this is the equation of a straight line on a plane. Any point on this line satisfies our system of equations.\n\nIf we want to find some **particular solution** of the system, we simply choose some particular value of $y$ and find the corresponding value of $x$ on the line. For example, if we choose $y=0,$ then the corresponding value is $x=-5$ and the particular solution is $x=-5,$ $y=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107080 #edb/ref 1065 3153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107080 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0c91df0b-40c7-5a65-9fda-5d2d6438dfa3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107080 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107081 #edb/ref 1064 "Given a $2 \\times 2$ system of linear equations, we have the following possibilities for the number of solutions:\n\n- The system has exactly *one solution* if and only if the respective coefficient matrix is non-singular (meaning that the determinant is not zero). Such systems are called **consistent independent**.\n\n- The system has *infinitely many solutions* if the second row in the standard echelon form contains only $0$'s. These systems are called **consistent dependent**.\n\n- The system has *no solution* if the second row in the standard echelon form contains only $0$'s on the left-hand side and a non-zero element on the right-hand side. These are called **inconsistent** systems.\n\n![](images/16/1692fbea298339ff839cf2f14986d51af45d1ec72b2b7d0de179336bdef40c0e.png)\n\n**Note:** A system of linear equations of the form \n![](images/cb/cb397dd01794f28ff11c01079e287d63175e439c8bbaa85cd02dc61689780fa8.svg)\nwhere we have only $0$'s on the right-hand side, is called **homogeneous**. A homogeneous system always has at least one solution, namely, the solution $x=0$ and $y=0.$ Or, in vector form, $\\langle 0,0 \\rangle.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107081 #edb/ref 1065 3154 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107081 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b885f832-9e76-5008-a764-7375b1c25e35" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107081 #edb/ref 1067 "The Number of Solutions of a 2x2 System" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107090 #edb/ref 1064 "Given a matrix $A,$ an **eigenvector** of $A$ is a *non-zero* vector $\\mathbf{v}$ such that \n\n$$\nA \\mathbf{v} = \\lambda \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nfor some scalar $\\lambda \\in \\mathbb{R}.$ The scalar $\\lambda$ is called the **eigenvalue** corresponding to the eigenvector $\\mathbf{v}.$\n\nFor example, consider the matrix \n![](images/63/63e2c65a23a0680412a14084d543b6cc19c0529fd80863b09c99987d309eaa4f.svg) \nand the vector ![](images/b7/b7811b74d8d3075357925864b5be27f57b292dadca9c04d222954baa1e66ba2a.svg) Computing the product $A \\cdot \\mathbf{v},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe've discovered that $A {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}} = {\\color{red}-2} {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}.$ Therefore,\n\n- the vector ![](images/13/13a28e543647ede7a88b30fb652bd7fba6c9adb8ee3142161a102de947ef2d2a.svg) is an *eigenvector* of $A,$ and\n\n- the scalar $\\color{red}\\lambda=-2$ is the *eigenvalue* corresponding to the eigenvector ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}.$\n\n**Caution:** Remember that an eigenvector must be a *non-zero* vector. This is very important." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107090 #edb/ref 1065 3557 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107090 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1014a8e8-38ce-568b-86d7-22f32ef8f697" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107090 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107097 #edb/ref 1064 "Sometimes, a system of equations might not have any solutions. For example, consider the following augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 10\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is\n![](images/e8/e8c64d8be247afd3e8ab02abc69e33635ef0234dbcbbc60f42379021a39a58a2.svg)\n\nThe final equation, $0x+0y=10,$ has no solution. It simplifies to $0=10,$ which is a false statement.\n\nSo, there is no pair $x,y$ that satisfies both equations in the system. Consequently, the overall system has no solution." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107097 #edb/ref 1065 3901 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107097 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "20c281f5-a455-581a-a7f7-fec6cfaa114e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107097 #edb/ref 1067 "Singular Systems with No Solution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107098 #edb/ref 1064 "Eigenvectors and eigenvalues can be interpreted geometrically.\n\nFirst, recall that if the matrix ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) is the standard matrix of some linear transformation ![](images/80/80d488fc9d8168cecdf882f1aa178325bb36bd387dd9f86f2b0cd98a5c54f4dc.svg) then the product ![](images/dc/dcc52971e9a7e86ebbe8bf972c562fce13d389f9261b69cba05dcabb1426b9dc.svg) gives the image of ![](images/4f/4fa2300af121b336bb0d7845b8e2b3e34be5a517a1bca45b4ec7b6514b2c544d.svg) under the action of ![](images/a1/a1af41bdb2f2fe410c6f8e02d1a2a0048f0aa470ceef4508753090444ca039be.svg) The image of ![](images/4f/4fa2300af121b336bb0d7845b8e2b3e34be5a517a1bca45b4ec7b6514b2c544d.svg) under the action of ![](images/c1/c15eeb9e7a3ff525fbdcd2f175622bd034f0b34899224f9ce2e08a5300d625de.svg) can appear quite different from the original vector ![](images/92/9221d4dc55c1df8da019956ea23f9d4f9ff3f5b04921ec2158804a31a50e636b.svg)\n\nHowever, for an eigenvector ![](images/77/771507e21f2935d77160257f7d56c3f5fcef1d0a114ac8a38c373c49790dd854.svg) with corresponding eigenvalue ![](images/4a/4a2f3891f0b4c8b852767d556e1607028035219b70ab83b4910e95159a930b9c.svg) we get\n\n![](images/ee/eed93a562368727b490c2b63d14f46580dcd0439ac90df23a23f75a31de9e971.svg)\n\nIn other words, when the matrix ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) is applied to an eigenvector ![](images/b2/b2766af4a0fe79e81fbd5ac113f0446ce1d111def401abbf09ced1ee62ca1cf1.svg) the image of ![](images/77/771507e21f2935d77160257f7d56c3f5fcef1d0a114ac8a38c373c49790dd854.svg) is a scaled version of the original vector ![](images/77/771507e21f2935d77160257f7d56c3f5fcef1d0a114ac8a38c373c49790dd854.svg), and the scale factor is ![](images/44/44db815dcaf0a9d405026460a655495e055df7e55a785f542f8920ca90af5418.svg)\n\nTo demonstrate, consider once again the following matrix:\n\n![](images/43/43e76f5a5e889ec18a6893be8cdbf4837d3dedfab149a0981ce6619a62718170.svg)\n\nWe've already seen that ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) has an eigenvector ![](images/fe/fef256ade4f52003da67fac5af68ff4b0d4e7f04ef55e72bb0279bdc8f1e8c3c.svg) with corresponding eigenvalue ![](images/b5/b5cdbd2f286044241daca2565a1ab7f70196ecc2cf6503965d9a820faad9b06e.svg) So we have\n\n![](images/2d/2da94bfdde0e3650ded2622748ead5a4c09cabf610f9d181d48ae158d9bd0986.svg)\n\nPlotting the vector ![](images/77/771507e21f2935d77160257f7d56c3f5fcef1d0a114ac8a38c373c49790dd854.svg) and its image ![](images/ab/abe8027351c1645f4c582440b8b536f48f3ec35d008570f3432f322fa468e861.svg) in the plane, we get the following picture.\n\n![](images/9a/9aca41e0597b0b1698e8ec24da9966fd9b7e5fee3951081d8abdb0cc0caaac7b.png)\n\nIn general, when we apply a matrix ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) to an eigenvector ![](images/b2/b2766af4a0fe79e81fbd5ac113f0446ce1d111def401abbf09ced1ee62ca1cf1.svg) the resulting vector ![](images/be/befecb9d885ee3bbbb7313401ca3671428e4aced68a4bfdb4b2fc9a005ea2b43.svg) always lies on the same line as ![](images/1f/1f3d1a60926323e963d7563529669e5cf8210771c8cbb0379f7a7d90e7c6aeec.svg)\n\nIf ![](images/8c/8c453358dacec49655b560be52a99066e3cab1d34cfbdbdbaa7bd74d868fef82.svg) then the matrix stretches the vector. For ![](images/ea/ea6d742051f82ff2f258e08ed09c3b3dc6d4c7f9bbbe40fea718aec8183dc44f.svg) the matrix compresses the vector. In addition, a negative eigenvalue ![](images/c9/c91cda6703c5767aaafeca807b07e466f160cb0ade84472ade5fa85d71a5713e.svg) means that ![](images/77/771507e21f2935d77160257f7d56c3f5fcef1d0a114ac8a38c373c49790dd854.svg) is flipped in the opposite direction as well as scaled." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107098 #edb/ref 1065 3963 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107098 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "cdf6ca75-e37b-552a-a106-ee8ac2f36a61" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107098 #edb/ref 1067 "The Geometric Interpretation of Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115390 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115390 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048999 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115390 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115775 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115390 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115776 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115390 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262878 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115390 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262897 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115390 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262900 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115390 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186302170 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [